    {
      "slug": "the-jack-pine",
      "title": "The Jack Pine",
      "year": 1916,
      "imageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/The_Jack_Pine%2C_by_Tom_Thomson.jpg",
      "imageWidth": 1200,
      "imageHeight": 1105,
      "story": "The Jack Pine is a well-known oil painting by Canadian artist Tom Thomson. A representation of the most broadly distributed pine species in Canada, it is considered an iconic image of the country's landscape, and is one of the country's most widely recognized and reproduced artworks.",
      "facts": [
        "Beginning in 1913, Thomson annually stayed in Algonquin Park from the spring until the autumn, often working as a guide while also fishing and painting for his own pleasure.",
        "It was there, on Grand Lake with the hills near Carcajou Bay in the background, that Thomson made the oil sketch in 1916 that he would use for the final painting in 1917."
      ],
      "wikipediaUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Jack_Pine",
